  "task_id": "d01",
  "request_sha256": "bd3d8c5ff64aad8439a4e945d7404225e896c68b9005bbef79e5990d923426d3",
  "proof": "by\n  intro G H g h f hf a b c d ha hb hc hd\n  let F : Theory.Hom g h := ⟨f, hf⟩\n  exact Theory.kernel_mul F\n    (Theory.kernel_mul F (x := a) (y := b) ha hb)\n    (Theory.kernel_mul F (x := c) (y := d) hc hd)\n",
  "bridge": "Bundle the raw function f and its multiplication-preservation hypothesis hf as F : Theory.Hom g h. The definition Theory.kernel F x reduces to f x = h.one, so ha, hb, hc, and hd supply kernel membership without additional obligations. Apply Theory.kernel_mul to a and b, apply it to c and d, and apply it once more to those two products. The resulting kernel statement reduces to the original balanced-product goal. No other candidate property is used. Packaging does not run Lean and is not Lean verification."
}

files.attempts/d02/001/submission.json:
{
  "task_id": "d02",
  "request_sha256": "04536f74c78357abe814fae377d7440624b7f4ae7147aaf0a9b53af07abfa03f",
  "proof": "by\n  intro G H g h f hf op hop x y z hx hy hz\n  let F : Theory.Hom g h := ⟨f, hf⟩\n  rw [hop (op x y) z, hop x y]\n  exact Theory.kernel_mul F hz (Theory.kernel_mul F hy hx)\n",
  "bridge": "Bundle the raw function f and multiplication hypothesis hf as F : Theory.Hom g h. By the definition of Theory.kernel, hx, hy, and hz provide kernel membership for x, y, and z. The first use of Theory.kernel_mul combines hy and hx to obtain membership for g.mul y x; the second combines hz with that result to obtain membership for g.mul z (g.mul y x). Rewriting both occurrences of op with hop turns the original target into exactly that kernel membership, which unfolds to the requested equality in the original vocabulary. The only candidate property used is Theory.kernel_mul. Packaging alone does not establish Lean verification."
}

